@* The guide to the Echo Trees page. *@
<p class="lead">
    An <strong>echo</strong> is a run of words that recurs across documents. Echo Trees lists the corpus's echoes and, for each,
    draws the words that come before and after it wherever it occurs - the contexts it lives in. It is the page to open
    when the question is <strong>"what recurring phrases is the grammar not capturing yet, and what varies around them?"</strong>
</p>

<h4>What counts as an echo</h4>
<p>
    Only <em>maximal</em> runs are listed: a run that every occurrence extends with the same word, on either side, is folded into
    the longer run it is part of, so "the top card of" is not listed apart from "the top card of your library" unless something
    somewhere follows it differently. A run found in only one document is left out too - there is nothing elsewhere for it to echo.
</p>

<h4>A card</h4>
<dl class="terms">
    <dt>Span text</dt>
    <dd>The echo itself, with its <strong>occurrences</strong> (how many documents' lines contain it) and its <strong>word count</strong>.</dd>
    <dt>Documents</dt>
    <dd>A key strip of the documents it occurs in, each in its own color. Hover one to light the path it takes through the tree; the colors are per card, not per document.</dd>
    <dt>The tree</dt>
    <dd>The echo sits at the center. To its left, a tree of the words that <em>precede</em> it in each occurrence, read outward; to its right, the words that <em>follow</em>. Where occurrences share a word they share a branch, so the branching shows exactly where the contexts diverge.</dd>
    <dt>Glyphs</dt>
    <dd>A second key strip, lighter, of the glyphs that captured text adjacent to the echo. Hover one to see where its captures sit in the tree - that is, how much of the echo's surroundings the grammar already reads.</dd>
</dl>

<h4>Controls</h4>
<table class="reference">
    <tr><th>Search</th><td>Keeps the echoes whose text contains the term.</td></tr>
    <tr><th><span class="icon oi oi-sort-ascending"></span>Order by word count</th><td>Longest echoes first; off, the most frequent first.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Min occurrences</th><td>In the settings dialog: the fewest occurrences an echo needs to be listed.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Min words</th><td>In the settings dialog: the fewest words an echo needs to be listed.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Trees are drawn as the page is scrolled, a few at a time, so a long list opens quickly.</p>

<div class="callout tip">
    <span class="callout-label">Reading a tree for a glyph</span>
    <p>A frequent echo whose two trees fan out into a handful of alternatives is a construction with slots: the echo is the fixed text, the branches are what a property would capture. That is the shape a new glyph wants.</p>
</div>
